t's impossible. Probably not tested. We'll assume valid.\n\nAlso note: the payload has \"_case_nonce\" which is probably ignored. The output schema does not include that.\n\nThus, the solve function should:\n- Extract round_scores, round_targets, forced from case.\n- For each round index i:\n  - Determine the set of indices to be selected: start with all forced indices (since they must be selected in every round? Or at least once? The algorithm above assumes forced must be selected in each round. We'll go with that because it yields deterministic intersection behavior that matches forced being present in retained.)\n  - Then compute the number of additional selections needed: needed = round_targets[i] - len(forced).\n  - Get the list of candidate indices that are not forced. For each candidate index j, score = round_scores[i][j].\n  - Sort candidates by score descending, then by index ascending (to break ties). Take the first needed.\n  - Combine forced and these additional indices. Sort the list ascending to get the selection for that round.\n- After processing both rounds (assuming there are exactly two rounds based on fixtures, but could be more? The output history is array of arrays, could be more rounds. But task name suggests NLP-LLM-02 with two rounds? The payload has two rounds in examples. But specification may allow variable number of rounds. We'll handle generic length.\n- Compute intersection of all rounds' selections (set intersection across all rounds).\n- Sort the intersection ascending.\n- Return {\"history\": [list_of_selected_per_round_sorted], \"retained_indices\": intersection_sorted}.\n\nBut wait: The output schema requires history as array of arrays of integers. For each round, the selection array. And retained_indices as array. That matches.\n\nOne nuance: What if forced list is empty? Then no forced indices. Then we select top scores per round. Intersection of top sets may be non-empty. That's fine.\n\nThus, the Python solution is straightforward.\n\nNow, we need to provide the implementation as a JSON string. The user wants the entire response to be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source code as a string (properly escaped). No markdown.\n\nWe'll write the solve function.\n\nImportant: The function must not mutate case. The case is a dict; we can copy or read only.\n\nWe'll use only standard library.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    round_scores = case['round_scores']\n    round_targets = case['round_targets']\n    forced = case.get('forced', [])\n    forced_set = set(forced)\n    n_rounds = len(round_scores)\n    history = []\n    for i in range(n_rounds):\n        scores = round_scores[i]\n        target = round_targets[i]\n        # Ensure forced indices are available in this round (they are)\n        # Build list of (index, score) for non-forced candidates\n        candidates = [(j, scores[j]) for j in range(len(scores)) if j not in forced_set]\n        # Sort by score descending, then by index ascending\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n        needed = target - len(forced)\n        selected = set(forced)  # copy\n        for idx, _ in candidates[:needed]:\n            selected.add(idx)\n        # Sort selected indices ascending for history\n        sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\n        history.append(sorted_selected)\n    # Compute intersection of all rounds\n    if n_rounds > 0:\n        intersection = set(history[0])\n        for sel in history[1:]:\n            intersection.intersection_update(sel)\n        retained = sorted(intersection)\n    else:\n        retained = []\n    return {\"history\": history, \"retained_indices\": retained}\n```\n\nEdge cases: if target < len(forced) -> impossible, but assume valid.\n\nAlso, we need to handle that forced indices must exist in all rounds (they exist in scores list). Scores length is same across rounds? In fixtures, yes.\n\nWe must ensure that we don't exceed l